# Cretli — comments in English

This is an open-source project. **All comments must be in English**: JSDoc block
comments, inline `//` comments, SCSS/CSS comments, and HTML comments.

## Rule

- Write every comment in English. Do not leave Polish comments in the code.
- When editing a file that still contains Polish comments, translate them to
  English as part of the change (best-effort within the touched file).
- This applies to `app_front/`, `lib/`, `server.js`, `public/`, `scripts/`,
  `tests/`, `docs/` and any new files.
- Keep comments meaningful: explain intent, trade-offs or non-obvious
  constraints — do not narrate what the code already says.

## Exceptions (NOT comments — do not touch here)

- **User-facing strings** are handled by the i18n system
  (`app_front/i18n/`, `lib/messages.js`). Polish UI text belongs in
  `app_front/i18n/pl.js`, English in `app_front/i18n/en.js`. Do not put Polish
  literals directly in components — use `t('key')`.
- **Agent prompts** (e.g. `config.js`, `fork-title.js`, `todo-agent.js`) are
  separate; default them to English for the models.
- Example values and identifiers (URLs, env names, option codes) stay as-is.

## Why

A consistent English codebase is required for external contributors and for
publishing on GitHub. Mixed-language comments are a maintenance smell and a
review blocker.
